{
  "image_name": "1b5aa40110e75d8c682acc46043663a3.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "This figure summarizes the proposed progression of rheumatoid arthritis from initiation to joint damage. It begins at the synovial membrane, where local injury, autocrine signaling, and infections appear to trigger a cytokine network involving mediators such as TNF, IL-6, and IL-23. Activated monocytes and infiltrating macrophages then promote modification of self antigens, including citrullination, leading to a key step: antigen-presenting cells activate adaptive immune cells.\n\nThe inflammatory phase shows presentation of self antigens to T cells, followed by expansion of helper T-cell subsets, especially TH1 and TH17, alongside B-cell activation and reduced regulatory T-cell control. This drives cytokine production, synovial invasion, and formation of lymphoid-like germinal centers.\n\nThe self-perpetuating stage depicts a feedback loop in which macrophages, T cells, and B cells maintain inflammation. Cartilage breakdown releases autoantigens and other matrix products such as proteoglycans and collagen-related fragments, while chondrocytes and collagenase contribute further tissue injury. Autoantibodies also appear to stimulate osteoclasts.\n\nThe final outcome is destructive joint disease: a genotoxic, protease-rich, cytokine-rich environment promotes pannus formation and osteoclast activation, ultimately causing erosion of bone and progressive tissue destruction.",
